WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.440
GUTTAG: I'm John Guttag.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.107
And today, it's my
pleasure to introduce

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:19.250
the speaker, Professor
Barbara Liskov, who

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.510
did not give me her bio.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.510
So I'm going to have to fake it.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:26.510
Barbara has been on the
faculty for, at least, longer

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.050
than I have.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.447
And I've been here
about 25 years

00:00:28.447 --> 00:00:29.780
or so I've been on the faculty--

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:30.620
26 maybe.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.025
She started when she was 15.

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:35.525
And--

00:00:35.525 --> 00:00:38.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.750
--is a well-respected expert
on programming languages,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.290
distributed systems,
fault tolerance systems,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.210
and a host of other topics.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.908
And I think with that, I'll
just turn it over to you--

00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:53.800
LISKOV: Thank you.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.707
GUTTAG: --and turn
off my microphone.

00:01:03.088 --> 00:01:03.630
LISKOV: Okay.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.720
So I'm going to talk today
about providing secure services

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.110
on the internet.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:13.990
And as I'm sure
you're all aware,

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.570
we are becoming more and
more dependent on information

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.900
that's stored online and
accessed over the internet.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.350
Truly critical information
is kept online.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.390
And it's very important
that this information

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.040
be stored securely.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.253
And I'm particularly interested
in two properties of security.

00:01:30.253 --> 00:01:32.170
So this is not exactly
the standard definition

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.160
of security.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.540
I'd like the
information that you

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.682
store to be accessible to
you, whatever you want it.

00:01:37.682 --> 00:01:39.640
And furthermore, I want
it to be uncorrupted so

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.800
that you can actually use it.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.770
And I'd like these
properties to be preserved

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.290
for you in spite of all
the kinds of problems

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.260
that can happen.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.830
And in particular, I'm
interested in hardware

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:57.032
failures, software errors,
and malicious attacks.

00:01:57.032 --> 00:01:58.990
And as you know, malicious
attacks particularly

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.030
are becoming more
and more something

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.540
that happens on the internet.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:05.950
Okay.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.410
So here's some examples of
the kind of online information

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.669
that I'm concerned about.

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:13.990
You might have a log of your
credit card entries being kept

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.820
by your credit card company.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.120
And it's very important
that that information not

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.020
be corrupted so that, for
example, the charges that you

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.980
think you have incurred
don't change mysteriously.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.260
Another example is a
key distribution service

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.140
that's used that allows you
to look up somebody's name

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.180
and get back a public
key that you can then

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.820
use to access some information.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.740
Yet another example is DNS,
the domain name server,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.610
which is the source of all
addressing on the internet.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:43.930
And if the DNA--

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.118
DNS name servers
are corrupted, you

00:02:46.118 --> 00:02:48.160
won't be able to find
anybody that you would like

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.870
to talk to on the internet.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.360
And then, another example is
simply a company's website.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.800
For example, recently
I read in the news

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.670
that the Staples website
had been hacked into.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:04.150
And everybody that went to
look at the Staples website

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.090
was immediately forwarded to
the website of a competitor.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.135
So you may not think
that the company website

00:03:10.135 --> 00:03:11.260
is such an important thing.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.000
But even there, corruption of
data through a malicious attack

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.440
can be a big problem.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.590
And as a matter of
fact, these problems

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.140
are becoming
increasingly evident.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.360
And what you see
in the news is just

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.970
a small sort of tip of the
iceberg of what's really

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:29.680
going on, because when
critical data is kept online,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.530
the owners of this data are
very interested in not having

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.110
you know that there's been
an attack that's caused

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.328
that data to become corrupted.

00:03:37.328 --> 00:03:38.870
Now, my concern in
this talk is going

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:40.970
to be what's called
data integrity.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.570
In other words, I want to make
sure your data is available.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:44.990
And it's uncorrupted.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.990
There are a whole bunch
of other security issues

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.730
that are obviously
also very important.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.370
For example, privacy--
you would like

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.920
to be sure that your
credit card logs

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.710
can't be looked
at by people that

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.710
aren't supposed to see them,
and access control, the ability

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:02.433
to control who has the right
to look at certain information.

00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:03.850
I'm not going to
talk about those.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.840
So as I mentioned
before, there are

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:12.530
sort of three main reasons why
integrity might be compromised.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.220
First of all,
hardware might fail.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.160
But in addition, there can
be bugs in your software.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.630
And finally, we can
have malicious attacks.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:28.040
And the way that you survive
these kinds of problems

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.850
is by replication.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.900
So replication--
the basic idea is

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.060
that if one of the
copies of the information

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.262
is hacked into or
destroyed, you can still

00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:38.720
provide access to
it, because there

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.148
are all those other copies.

00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:41.690
And of course, for
this to work, this

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.180
means that the replicas that
make up the replicated system

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.120
are going to have to carry
out some kind of protocol that

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.550
guarantees that they are
in sync with one another

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.490
so that the information you
get from the backup copy

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.370
if the primary fails is
up to date with respect

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.680
to what the primary has stored.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.720
Now, traditional
fault-tolerance,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.690
which was a topic of
great research interest

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.050
in the '80s, was concerned
with a particular sort

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.570
of replication algorithm
that was designed to define--

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.930
to survive what are
called failstop failures.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.547
So failstop failures
are a very simple kind

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:17.630
of failure compared to
what we'll be talking about

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.910
in this talk,
because the idea here

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.880
is that when a failure
happens, the way it manifests

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.190
itself is that
things simply stop.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.305
So for example, a computer--

00:05:28.305 --> 00:05:29.930
something goes wrong
with the hardware.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.860
And the computer simply
stops functioning.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.170
Or well, so that's an example
of a failstop failure.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:41.600
And these failures are
relatively easy to cope with,

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.942
because you essentially
have to worry only

00:05:44.942 --> 00:05:46.400
about the question
of, what do I do

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.000
if I can't hear from somebody?

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.180
And you don't have to
worry about the problem of,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.040
I'm hearing from this entity.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.410
But it's not telling
me the right stuff.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.680
So in the '80s,
people concentrated

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:58.695
on failstop failures.

00:05:58.695 --> 00:06:00.570
And there were actually
two reasons for this.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.350
One is at that time, hardware
wasn't all that reliable.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.440
And so this was considered
to be sort of the major thing

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.250
you had to be concerned about.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:12.890
And the other reason was
that this seemed hard enough.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.650
We didn't understand how to
build replication systems that

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.610
could survive even
these simple failures.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.610
And so tackling the much
more difficult failures

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.100
that I'm going to
talk about today

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.410
was sort of way beyond what
we could think about doing.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.680
To survive simple
failstop failures,

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.640
you require 2f+1 replicas.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:31.350
And I'm going to show
you why that's true.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:32.850
For example, to
survive one failure,

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.350
you're going to
need three replicas.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.585
And then, when you
run your operations,

00:06:39.585 --> 00:06:41.210
each operation is
going to have to sort

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.620
of check in with at least
two of those replicas.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.635
And we're going
to have to ensure

00:06:46.635 --> 00:06:49.010
that these operations are
totally ordered so that if you,

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:51.770
say, modify the
replicated service twice,

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.620
the second replication-- the
second modification doesn't

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.200
sort of forget what
happened in the first one.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.280
And that's going to require
a two-phase protocol.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.310
So in the case of
failstop failures,

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.470
you can get by with
2f+1 1 replicas.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.810
And you can carry out
a two-phase protocol.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:09.320
And I have a little example
here to show you why--

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.610
sort of what's going on and
why you need 2f+1 replicas.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.650
So for example,
this green circle

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.970
might represent the operation
that executes right now.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.950
And it happens to
manage to be carried out

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.620
at these two blue replicas.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.310
And then, at some
time in the future,

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.638
another operation
is carried out.

00:07:28.638 --> 00:07:30.680
And for some reason, it
isn't able to communicate

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.630
with this replica.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.910
But instead-- I guess
I should be using--

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:36.410
I see.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.450
Yes, that wasn't really
very effective, was it?

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:40.370
All right.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:43.070
Now, if I just knew
how to use this--

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:46.568
MAN 1: Just try pointing to
the red part of the screen.

00:07:46.568 --> 00:07:47.110
LISKOV: Okay.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.110
MAN 2: Yeah, you're
pointing at yourself.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:51.510
LISKOV: Oh, okay.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.970
So in this green circle is
where the first operation

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:54.840
was executed.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.940
And for some reason, it
only executed those two

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.470
blue replicas.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.297
And then, at a later time, the
red operation was executed.

00:08:02.297 --> 00:08:04.380
And for some reason, it
wasn't able to communicate

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.380
with this replica but was
able to be carried out

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.170
with these two replicas.

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:11.940
The point is that because
we had 2f+1 replicas and--

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.860
or rather three replicas
in this example--

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.530
and because each operation
happened at two of them,

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.020
we can be certain that
the two operations

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.682
overlap in one place.

00:08:20.682 --> 00:08:22.890
And that's going to be
sufficient for us to make sure

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.380
that the later
operation reflects

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.330
the effects of the
earlier operation,

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.430
provided we carry out
the proper protocol.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.700
So for example, supposing
this was a key distribution

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.929
center and system, and what was
happening was for some reason,

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:39.809
two entries were being made for
the very same name and a very

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:41.409
close period of time.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:44.460
So the green operation inserted
some public key for that name.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.310
And then, a few seconds
later, the red one

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.900
inserted a different
later public key

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.460
for the very same name.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.440
Or rather, let me rephrase that.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:53.940
[LAUGHS]

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.878
Supposing that the first
one did it for Name 1

00:08:55.878 --> 00:08:57.420
and the second one
did it for Name 2,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:58.800
and what's going on
in these operations

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.967
is you're rewriting the
database to reflect the most

00:09:00.967 --> 00:09:02.820
current set of entries,
you want to be sure

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.880
that that second
operation writes back

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.950
the right result for
the first operation,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.930
as well as the right
result for itself.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.570
So that's an example of why
we need a two-phase protocol.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.420
We have to have one phase that
finds out what happened before

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.000
and a second phase that reflects
that, as well as the changes

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.810
that are being made.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:23.070
I'm not going to go into
the various algorithms

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.950
that people invented
to carry out

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:27.870
this kind of simple protocol.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.600
I just wanted to point out
primarily that you need 2f+1

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.670
replicas and a
two-phase protocol.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.170
Yes.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.460
AUDIENCE: Why is it
that f+2 won't work?

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.150
LISKOV: That f+2 won't work--

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:39.930
[LAUGHS]

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:44.040
--so if we had f+2, then in
the case of two failures,

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.470
we'd have four replicas.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:50.970
Now, so then, you have to
ask the question, well,

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.475
how many replicas are
involved in each carrying

00:09:53.475 --> 00:09:55.350
out of the operation,
because the requirement

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.230
is going to be that you
have these overlapping sets

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.090
in each subsequent operation?

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.755
So with four replicas,
you would only

00:10:01.755 --> 00:10:03.630
be able to survive one
failure, because you'd

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.970
have to carry out
the operations at--

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.950
carrying out at two
replicas wouldn't

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.840
be sufficient to get that
overlapping property.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:11.340
So--

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.980
AUDIENCE: So you know
which one's failed,

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:14.730
though, don't you?

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.750
LISKOV: No, you don't
necessarily know that.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:17.150
AUDIENCE: Oh, okay.

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:17.400
LISKOV: Okay.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:19.150
What you know is that
these are the ones I

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:20.910
was able to talk to right now.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.605
And you would like to be sure
that at least one of them

00:10:23.605 --> 00:10:25.230
knows about the most
recent things that

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:26.190
have happened in the past.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.498
AUDIENCE: I thought
because it was failstop

00:10:27.498 --> 00:10:28.665
you know which one's failed.

00:10:28.665 --> 00:10:31.200
LISKOV: No, because in
the case of a system

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.602
with-- in the internet,
you can't really

00:10:33.602 --> 00:10:35.060
tell a difference
between a failure

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.305
and a lack of a response.

00:10:36.305 --> 00:10:36.930
AUDIENCE: Okay.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.050
LISKOV: Okay.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:38.550
Okay.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.920
So all right.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.560
So that's how things
were in the '80s.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.950
And the problem is that those
failstop failures are only

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:48.780
the sort of the
tip of the iceberg.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:52.200
And what we'd really like
is replication algorithms

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.700
to survive much more
serious failures.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.420
Those failstop
failures really only

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:01.560
correspond to hardware failures,
where the system effectively

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.910
crashes.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:07.050
And it doesn't
match what happens

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.810
when you have a software
error, which can sometimes

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.620
cause your system to fail
and yet continue to run.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.750
And it also doesn't
cover what happens

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:18.300
when there's a malicious attack,
like that example of Staples

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.758
that I mentioned
a few minutes ago.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.920
So those kinds of failures
can cause your system

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:29.180
to stop acting properly and
yet continue to carry out

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:33.140
a protocol that can fool the
other replicas into thinking

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:35.910
that that node is
perfectly okay.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:37.730
And what happens when
you start to think

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:42.320
about failures like this is
it becomes very Byzantine.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.750
You sort of have to think
that everybody's scheming

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:48.290
to make things misbehave in
the sort of worst possible way.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.380
Whatever answer you get
from somebody is a lie.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.090
And therefore, you
have to have replica--

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:55.160
you have to have
replication techniques that

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:58.430
are capable of surviving
in spite of all those lies,

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:02.040
provided there haven't
been too many of them.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:02.540
Okay.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.760
And these are, in fact, the
majority of faults today.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.790
They may not manifest themselves
as Byzantine failures.

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:09.407
But there's no
doubt that failures

00:12:09.407 --> 00:12:11.240
due to software errors
and malicious attacks

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.770
are the primary reason that
we have failures today.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:17.610
Okay.

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:19.110
So what I'm going
to do today is I'm

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.630
going to talk about how you
can survive Byzantine failures.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.672
I'm going to describe a
new replication algorithm

00:12:23.672 --> 00:12:26.220
to you that survives
Byzantine failures

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:29.012
and has very good
survival properties.

00:12:29.012 --> 00:12:30.970
And furthermore, it's
practical, performs well.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.760
And I'll show you a little
bit of performance evaluation

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.470
results to show you
what's going on.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:37.960
All right.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:44.560
So the-- in contrast to the
simple failstop failures

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.275
that I talked
about a minute ago,

00:12:46.275 --> 00:12:47.650
when you have
Byzantine failures,

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.780
you require more
replicas and more phases

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:52.360
in your replication protocol.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.040
So you require 3f+1 replicas
to survive f failures.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:58.870
So for example, to
survive one failure,

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.570
you now need four
replicas, not three.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.150
And furthermore, your
replication protocol

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:07.210
has to have three phases
in it, not two phases.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:08.020
Okay.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:11.410
And when we started this work,
it was already known that you

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:14.260
needed 3f+1 replicas to
survive that failures.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:16.780
That had been proved earlier.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.810
Our algorithm is more
efficient than algorithms

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.310
that people have
invented before.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.620
So-- and I don't know about
a minimality proof that

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:25.150
would show you
require three phases,

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.910
though I certainly haven't been
able to think of a way of doing

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.270
it with fewer.

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:29.770
Okay.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.020
Now, the algorithm that I'm
going to describe for you

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.780
provides what's called
state machine replication.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.370
And this is what
you really want.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.440
The idea is that
you have a service.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:42.220
An arbitrary service typically
provides access to its services

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:45.160
by means of operations
that clients can call.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.350
So for example,
in a file system,

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:49.193
you can ask to read
and write entire files.

00:13:49.193 --> 00:13:50.860
You can also read and
write directories,

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.300
rename, and so forth.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:53.800
And in a key
distribution center,

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:55.660
you could store a new pair.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:57.650
You could read a pair and so on.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:01.540
So anything that you think about
can be phrased in this form.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.190
And really what I'm talking
about here is data abstraction.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.210
So we're talking about
arbitrary services

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.877
that provide operations
that allow users

00:14:09.877 --> 00:14:12.460
to make use of their services
at the level of abstraction that

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:15.920
makes sense for
that application.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.520
And one of the nice things
about state machine replication,

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:20.478
in addition to the fact
that it sort of matches

00:14:20.478 --> 00:14:22.420
the way that people
really do their business,

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.690
is that it will allow us
to provide service in spite

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:27.520
of Byzantine-faulty clients.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:30.520
Now, a client-- this is the
node that's trying to use

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:31.330
the service--

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:34.300
if the client is storing, for
example, bad data in a file,

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.010
we can't do anything about that.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:37.720
Access controls the
mechanism that you

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:39.610
would use to try and
at least guarantee

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:42.640
that whoever is writing that
file has the right to write it.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.310
But what we can do with
state machine replication

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:47.710
is we can make sure that the
client doesn't sort of get

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.650
stopped halfway through and
leave the system in such

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:52.660
a bad state with its
invariance broken

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.430
and so forth that
you're sort of not

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:56.030
able to continue your business.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.660
So it's really very nice to be
able to provide state machine

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.128
replication rather than the
simpler thing that many people

00:15:01.128 --> 00:15:02.920
have worked on, which
is simply the ability

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.057
to read and write single words.

00:15:05.057 --> 00:15:06.640
With reading and
writing single words,

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:08.920
you have to implement, in
some sense, the higher level

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.440
semantics at the
client machine itself.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.390
And then, you interact
with the service

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.190
by doing reads and
writes in sequence.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:19.500
Okay.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:21.780
So here's basically-- I'm
just going to try and show you

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.738
here what it is we have
to do in this algorithm

00:15:23.738 --> 00:15:25.210
in order to make it work.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.660
So in state machine
replication, what you do is

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.880
you assume that the
operations are deterministic.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.880
In fact, you require
them to be deterministic.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.370
And furthermore, you make
sure that the replicas all

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:38.770
start up at the beginning
of time in the same state.

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:41.820
And then, assuming that
the replicas execute

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.110
the same requests
in the same order,

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.480
OK, you will know
that correct replicas

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:49.610
will produce identical results.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:53.030
So the key thing here
is this thing here,

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:55.070
because what this is
telling me is what

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:57.290
my protocol has to accomplish.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:00.530
I have to be sure in my
protocol to guarantee

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:04.790
that all the non-faulty replicas
execute the same operations

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:05.700
in the same order.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.464
AUDIENCE: Is it obvious
what deterministic

00:16:07.464 --> 00:16:09.388
should mean in this context?

00:16:09.388 --> 00:16:12.262
It's not obvious to me.

00:16:12.262 --> 00:16:13.470
LISKOV: It's the usual thing.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:15.710
In other words, if you
call that operation

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:19.370
with the same arguments,
I'm sure there's

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:22.618
a trick here somewhere,
okay, that it should

00:16:22.618 --> 00:16:23.660
produce the same results.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:30.410
So for example, file systems
are sort of deterministic,

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:32.510
except that whenever
you read a file,

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:35.390
you get this time last
modified or time last read that

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:35.930
gets sent.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:36.597
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:16:36.597 --> 00:16:38.360
LISKOV: That's a
non-deterministic effect.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.400
And so to do a
simple file system,

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.560
we're going to have to play
some sort of trick that

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:43.820
makes sure that we--

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:48.300
that all the replicas will
set the same time last read.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:48.800
Okay.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.770
So there's nothing
unusual going on here.

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:55.994
Determinism is the same old
thing we're accustomed to.

00:16:55.994 --> 00:16:58.200
To rephrase that, if
you're in the same state

00:16:58.200 --> 00:16:59.752
and you operate the
same X operation,

00:16:59.752 --> 00:17:01.460
you should come out
with the same result.

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:02.370
AUDIENCE: At some
level of abstraction.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.412
LISKOV: At some level of
abstraction-- definitely

00:17:04.412 --> 00:17:05.910
at a level of abstraction.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:06.410
Okay.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:10.310
So the key is going to be
that thing I highlighted.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:13.800
That's what the protocol
has to accomplish.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:14.560
And it's not easy.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:17.550
Okay.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.240
So what are the clients do?

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.310
I'm going to start
talking about them.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:24.930
First of all, they carry out a
little protocol of their own.

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:27.660
They send their request
to all the replicas.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:31.770
And then, they wait for
f+1 identical replies.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:35.220
And the reason that
works is because we're

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:38.500
talking about a system that
can survive f failures.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:39.000
Okay.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.970
So you have to assume that
there are most f faulty replicas

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.800
in the system at this moment.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.800
So f of these replies
might be total y's.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:50.070
But one of them is coming
from a non-faulty replica.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:54.720
And therefore, we know that
we have one replica that said,

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:55.600
this is the answer.

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:58.538
And we can rely on that.

00:17:58.538 --> 00:18:00.305
Yeah.

00:18:00.305 --> 00:18:02.347
AUDIENCE: If one of them
is fault and one of them

00:18:02.347 --> 00:18:05.247
is good at this stage
of the protocol, how do

00:18:05.247 --> 00:18:06.010
you know which--

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.390
LISKOV: I haven't
showed you the protocol.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:07.620
Okay.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:09.720
All I'm showing you is
what the client does.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.300
So yes, we have to ensure
that the answer given us

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:15.878
by that one non-faulty
replica is a true answer.

00:18:15.878 --> 00:18:17.670
So we're going to need
a protocol that will

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:19.500
ensure that that's correct.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.460
Now, remember it's
identical answers.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:23.550
So we're going to--

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:25.350
we can't rely on
any f of these guys.

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:28.350
But we can rely on the group
of them, because one of them

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.700
is not telling us a lie.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:31.380
Okay.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:33.130
But I haven't showed
you the protocol yet.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:35.781
I just showed you
what the client does.

00:18:35.781 --> 00:18:38.550
Okay.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.050
Okay.

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:40.175
So what do the replicas do?

00:18:40.175 --> 00:18:43.032
Well, they have to carry
out a protocol that

00:18:43.032 --> 00:18:45.490
ensures that those replies from
the non-faulty replicas are

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:46.330
actually correct.

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:54.740
And that requires that enough
replicas process each request,

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:58.280
right, to ensure that, you
know, the non-faulty replicas

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.560
are processing the same
request in the same order.

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:02.810
And in particular, to sort
of answer your question,

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:06.020
a non-faulty replica will never
send an answer to a request

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:09.490
that it hasn't achieved by
carrying out this protocol.

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:10.040
Okay.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:12.540
So it doesn't really matter
what those liars said,

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:13.880
because we know we can rely--

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:16.568
if we have f+1 answers, at
least one must be honest.

00:19:16.568 --> 00:19:18.110
And the protocol
was enough to ensure

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:19.402
that that was the right answer.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:24.440
Okay.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:27.290
So now, I'm going to go into
the heart of the protocol

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:31.160
and tell you in a sort of a
hand wavy sense, you know,

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:33.150
how we manage to do this.

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.060
So the first thing
I want to point out

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:37.430
is that we are using a
particular kind of protocol

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.500
kind of primary backup scheme.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:43.430
And what that means is
that at any moment in time,

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:47.470
the group of replicas as a whole
is in what's called a view.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:50.460
A view in this system
is a very simple thing.

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:52.940
It just means that in this
view, one of the replicas

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:53.900
acts as the primary.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.640
And the remainder are backups.

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:58.280
And actually, we achieve
this in a very simple way.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:00.530
A view has a view number
associated with it.

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:03.290
And the primary
is simply the node

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:08.090
whose replica ID modulo
the view number is 1.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:11.250
You know, so we choose it
in a very simple minded way.

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:11.750
Okay.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.167
So the main point
is that the view

00:20:13.167 --> 00:20:15.290
designates who the primary is.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:17.690
And the primary
is the one that's

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.540
going to determine the
ordering of the requests.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:22.280
The advantage of
a primary scheme

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:24.380
is that one guy gets
to choose the order.

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.880
Otherwise, we'd have to have
some sort of agreement protocol

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:28.547
in which we'd all be
choosing the orders

00:20:28.547 --> 00:20:29.660
and somehow voting on it.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:31.050
And that's more expensive.

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.840
So the primary
chooses the order.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:35.450
But the problem is in
a system like this,

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:37.470
the primary might be a liar.

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:39.350
And so the order
that it's choosing

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.360
may not be the proper order.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:44.660
And therefore, the backups
have to ensure that the primary

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:45.950
behaves correctly.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:49.090
So the backups are checking up
on what the primary is doing.

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:51.590
If they don't like the order
that the primary is suggesting,

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:53.053
they won't go along with it.

00:20:53.053 --> 00:20:54.470
And then, that
will trigger what's

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:58.400
called a view change, which
will go to a higher view number.

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:01.740
And a different node will become
the primary in that new view.

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.890
So the way we survive
failures in this system

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:05.540
is by doing these
view changes, which

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.490
change who the primary is.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:11.570
And the system will work as long
as no more than f replicas are

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:14.260
faulty.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:15.020
Okay.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:20.960
So now, the system is
based on two notions

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.088
of quorums and certificates.

00:21:23.088 --> 00:21:25.130
A quorum-- I actually
showed you a quorum earlier

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:26.840
when I talked about
the simpler system.

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:28.140
Yes, Ron.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:30.560
AUDIENCE: I want to make
sure I understood the model.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.370
Are you talking also
about client failures

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:37.090
or malicious clients or
just malicious servers?

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:39.765
LISKOV: I'm really only talking
about malicious servers.

00:21:39.765 --> 00:21:41.890
AUDIENCE: The client tries
to mess up the database.

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:43.960
LISKOV: The client can mess
up the database in some ways

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:44.790
and not in others.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:47.830
Remember, it could write
bad data into the file.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:50.650
And we'd have to solve that
problem through access control.

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:54.130
But the client
isn't able to cause

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:57.130
file reads to misbehave
in some fundamental sense,

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:59.710
because it doesn't have the
ability to execute a file read.

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:02.320
It just is able to ask
for a file read to happen

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.110
or a file write.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:04.485
AUDIENCE: Do you
think the writes

00:22:04.485 --> 00:22:07.015
can cause inconsistencies
in ways that--

00:22:07.015 --> 00:22:08.640
LISKOV: He can't
cause inconsistencies.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.780
He could write bad
data to a file.

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.510
And this would show
up consistently

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:13.680
at all the replicas.

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:17.047
He can't cause a file to become
inconsistent with the replicas.

00:22:17.047 --> 00:22:19.380
And that's precisely what
you're getting out of the fact

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:22.930
that this is a state
machine replication system.

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:24.160
Okay.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:26.980
So we can sort of survive
faulty clients, but not

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.410
really, because of course,
it's very bad if you

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.310
write bad data to a file.

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:34.150
But that's no different
from an unreplicated system

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.160
in which somebody writes
bad data to a file.

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:39.700
Okay.

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:44.950
So in my earlier discussion of
the non-Byzantine failure case,

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:47.800
remember we had 2f+1 replicas.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:50.770
And every operation had to be
carried out at two of them,

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.270
or rather at f+1 of them.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:54.880
And that's what's
called a quorum.

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:57.100
So a quorum in these
systems is a set

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:59.710
of replicas that's big
enough that it overlaps

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.990
with the next set of
replicas that carries out

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:04.600
the next operation.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:08.170
Now, in this system, quorums
have to have at least 2f+1

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:09.250
replicas.

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:11.650
And to understand
why that's true,

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:16.300
we need to look at what happens
when you execute an operation.

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:18.130
And so again, what
I'm showing you here

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:21.110
is a system with one-- that
will survive one failure.

00:23:21.110 --> 00:23:22.690
So it's got four replicas in it.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:25.000
And we have our little
green operation there

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.650
that executed at
these three replicas.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:28.150
Okay.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.140
And so that was a quorum.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.060
And it succeeded in executing.

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.550
And then, we
executed our second--

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:40.230
doesn't really matter
which is which--

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:42.840
operation later, using
a different set of three

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:43.930
replicas.

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:44.430
Okay.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:49.470
Now, since we have 2f+1
replicas in a quorum,

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:54.600
this means two quorums will
intersect in f+1 replicas.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:55.170
Okay.

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:58.020
And that's very important,
because at any moment in time,

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:00.040
one replica could be faulty.

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:00.540
Okay.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:03.330
So that guarantees the quorums
always intersect in at least

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:05.280
one non-faulty replica.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.015
And that's what it is that
guarantees that whatever

00:24:08.015 --> 00:24:09.390
happened in the
earlier operation

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.460
can be reflected in
the later operation,

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:14.110
because that truth will
be carried through the one

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.390
non-faulty replica.

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:17.280
Okay.

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:19.380
Another way of looking
at this picture

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:20.910
is think about what
happened when we

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:23.310
executed this operation here.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:26.230
It looked like three
replicas carried it out.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:29.490
But this gray guy might
very well have been a liar.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:30.570
Okay.

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:33.030
And the blue guy that
we didn't hear from

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:36.030
might simply have been a
slow, you know, but non-faulty

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:37.120
replica.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:37.620
Okay.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.393
So the fact that we
got a quorum doesn't

00:24:39.393 --> 00:24:41.810
mean that everybody in the
quorum is telling us the truth.

00:24:41.810 --> 00:24:43.612
And so we have to have
big enough quorums

00:24:43.612 --> 00:24:44.820
to take care of that problem.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:49.730
AUDIENCE: Is the operation
initially to talk to everybody,

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:53.023
or do they talk to a
quorum like [INAUDIBLE]??

00:24:53.023 --> 00:24:54.690
LISKOV: They actually
talk to everybody.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:55.860
And I'm going to--
in the next slide,

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:57.943
I'm going to walk through
how we sort of carry out

00:24:57.943 --> 00:24:58.900
this protocol.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:24:59.400
Okay.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:00.703
So that's what a quorum is.

00:25:00.703 --> 00:25:03.120
And they're big enough so that
quorums always intersect in

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.830
at least one non-faulty replica.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:06.390
Okay.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.223
The other thing that
we use in the algorithm

00:25:08.223 --> 00:25:10.560
is what's called a
quorum certificate.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.380
And that's just
a set of messages

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:16.620
that a replica accumulates
from a quorum--

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:18.990
identical messages
from a quorum.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:20.940
So all messages
in the quorum will

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.180
agree on the same statement.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.722
And I'm going to show you what
these statements are like when

00:25:24.722 --> 00:25:26.890
I go to the next slide.

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:27.390
Okay.

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.030
So here's how we order requests.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:34.020
Here, we have a request
coming in from a client.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:37.382
And the client sends that
request to the primary.

00:25:37.382 --> 00:25:39.090
Now, in reality, the
client might send it

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:40.548
to some of the
other replicas also.

00:25:40.548 --> 00:25:42.090
But it goes to the primary.

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:44.460
And the primary is responsible
for choosing the sequence

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:45.310
number.

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:48.060
So the sequence number
for the requests

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:51.130
is going to be the order
in which they're executed.

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:52.710
So the primary
receives this request.

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:55.510
It says, this is going
to be request 123.

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:56.430
Okay.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.230
And it broadcasts
that information

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:00.000
to all the other replicas.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.910
So that's what's happening
in this part of the picture.

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:06.570
Now, this little thing down here
indicates that the whole thing

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.160
is encrypted.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:08.660
Okay.

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:11.510
And in fact, we use cryptography
on every single message that's

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:13.050
exchanged in this system.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.320
One thing that happens
is even this request, m,

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:17.330
that's coming from the
client is encrypted

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:21.200
with a secret key that
is known to the replicas

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:22.710
for that client.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.190
And here, we're
using the secret key

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:27.050
that the primary
uses to communicate

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:28.690
with the other
members of the group.

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:29.030
Okay.

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:31.238
In fact, we use what's called
an authenticator there,

00:26:31.238 --> 00:26:33.910
because there's a different
secret key for every member

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:35.160
of the group that the client--

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:36.470
the primary is talking to.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.630
And we're going to
broadcast this message.

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.210
And we're using secret
key cryptography here,

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:45.050
because it's much cheaper
than public key cryptography.

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.580
But these secret keys need
to be changed periodically.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:49.910
And so they're backed
up with public keys

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:53.160
and private keys that can be
used to exchange new keys.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:53.660
Okay.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:55.760
So the primary picks
the sequence number

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.195
for that message and
ships it off to everybody.

00:26:58.195 --> 00:26:59.570
And it's all
encrypted so that we

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:02.420
can make sure that when it
arrives, it's uncorrupted.

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:05.480
And it came from the
primary and so forth.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:06.200
Okay.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:09.170
Now, when a replica receives
one of those messages,

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:12.410
it carefully checks it over
to make sure that it likes it.

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:14.510
And it will never--
a correct replica

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:17.880
will never accept two messages
that pick the same sequence

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:21.500
message-- sequence number for
different client requests.

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:24.830
So if a replica ever
receives from a primary

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:28.190
another thing saying
here's another number 127,

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:30.290
it won't accept the second one.

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:30.858
Okay.

00:27:30.858 --> 00:27:32.525
And it also checks
other things about it

00:27:32.525 --> 00:27:35.150
and makes sure that the client
message is really uncorrupted

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:36.480
and so forth and so on.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:36.980
Yeah.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:39.022
AUDIENCE: Are the sequence
numbers global or just

00:27:39.022 --> 00:27:40.010
within a view?

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:41.015
LISKOV: They're global.

00:27:41.015 --> 00:27:41.640
They're global.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.140
So the view change is going to
have to preserve the sequence

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:46.170
numbers going forward.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.100
Okay.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:47.600
All right.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:49.520
So the primary has
chosen that number.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:52.370
And now, let's imagine that
it arrived at some replica.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:53.645
And the replica looked at it.

00:27:53.645 --> 00:27:54.770
And everything looked okay.

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:58.100
It had never received
a 127 before.

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:01.020
The client message was in
good condition and so forth.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:04.430
So then, what it does is it
immediately sends out messages

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:07.040
to every other
replica, saying, I'm

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:10.290
happy with that number
for this client request.

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:12.140
So that's what's going on here.

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:13.980
A replica is sending
out-- in fact,

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:16.820
what I'm showing you here is
various replicas are sending

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:17.840
out their acceptances.

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.330
Each replica is making its
decision independently.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.730
Each one is broadcasting
its decision

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:24.810
to the other replicas
in the group.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:27.440
And in this particular
picture, one of the replicas

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:29.550
is shown as being failed.

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.010
So it's not responding.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:33.663
And the primary is not bothering
to do this, because it already

00:28:33.663 --> 00:28:35.330
sort of said it
accepted it since it was

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:38.840
the one that chose the number.

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:39.590
Okay.

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:42.650
Now, the next thing that
happens is the replicas wait

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:44.840
for these acceptance messages.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:48.680
And they're going to try and
collect a quorum certificate

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:51.080
containing one--

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.150
you know, I select
number such and such

00:28:53.150 --> 00:28:58.690
for this client request and
the others, 2f of them, saying,

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:00.310
I accept that.

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:00.810
Okay.

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:02.610
So this is what I meant
by quorum certificate.

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:04.650
They're identical in the
sense that they're all

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:06.986
agreeing on the same statement.

00:29:06.986 --> 00:29:07.565
Okay.

00:29:07.565 --> 00:29:09.190
And of course, one
of these is probably

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:10.870
coming from the replica itself.

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:12.640
So it knows its own acceptance.

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:14.680
And it's waiting to hear from--

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:17.050
it heard from the primary to
sort of get itself started.

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:19.383
And it's waiting to hear from
the other-- a sufficiently

00:29:19.383 --> 00:29:21.920
large number of other
replicas that they also agree.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:22.420
Okay.

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:27.700
So then when it has that, we say
that the request is prepared,

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:29.110
okay, which means that--

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.600
simply that everybody
has managed--

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:34.780
that this replica has
managed to collect

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:39.640
enough messages that agree
that it has a quorum for that

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:40.280
prepare.

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:40.540
Yes.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:42.300
AUDIENCE: Could the primary
send a different message

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:43.180
to each backup?

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:44.170
LISKOV: Absolutely.

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:44.710
AUDIENCE: So you have to--

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:45.415
LISKOV: That's part
of the problem.

00:29:45.415 --> 00:29:47.735
AUDIENCE: --be able to create
the prepared certificate.

00:29:47.735 --> 00:29:48.610
LISKOV: That's right.

00:29:48.610 --> 00:29:49.520
Some of you will.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:51.460
You know, this replica might.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:53.560
But you need enough
of them to create--

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:56.440
each of them have to create
their own prepared certificate

00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:00.712
in order for us to actually
carry out the request.

00:30:00.712 --> 00:30:02.170
And what Mike is
getting at is sort

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:04.587
of what's really fundamentally
different about this system

00:30:04.587 --> 00:30:06.130
from the system we saw before.

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:07.720
Anybody might be lying.

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.970
The worst possible one
to lie is the primary,

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:14.170
because it can mislead the other
replicas in arbitrary ways.

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:16.960
And at this point,
all a node knows

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:18.940
is that things
looked okay to it.

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:20.710
But there hasn't been
any global agreement

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:22.030
yet that that's all right.

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:23.980
And so it wouldn't be
appropriate to execute

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:26.530
the message right now, because
you don't know for sure

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:28.520
that it's really
going to happen.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:31.100
So we have to go on.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:31.600
Okay.

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:33.590
So here we are.

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:36.670
We just found that we had
a prepared certificate

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:38.320
at some replica.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:39.850
So when a replica
has accumulated

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:43.150
a prepared certificate, then
it sends a message out saying,

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:44.420
I've got one.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:44.920
Okay.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:46.930
And it sends it to everybody.

00:30:46.930 --> 00:30:47.920
Okay.

00:30:47.920 --> 00:30:51.010
And again, each replica is
doing this independently.

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:52.510
And now, a replica
is waiting to see

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:54.550
whether it can't hear
from enough replicas

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:57.070
that they all are prepared.

00:30:57.070 --> 00:31:00.070
And when it has heard about--

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:05.170
when it receives a quorum
certificate for these prepares,

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:07.720
then it commits the operation.

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:10.510
Now, it knows that the
replica group as a whole

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:13.840
has agreed that this
is a good thing to do.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:14.740
Okay.

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:17.440
And so at that point, it's
ready to actually execute

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:21.370
the message, send the
answer back to the client.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:25.000
One thing is you don't
execute a request until you've

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:27.230
executed all earlier requests.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:30.070
And this particular
protocol is actually

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:32.090
operating asynchronously.

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:36.670
So there can be many messages in
the process of being processed.

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:41.150
And when you finally decide
that it's okay to do number 127,

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:43.410
you'll wait at that
point in case number 126

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.470
hadn't been done yet.

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:45.970
Yeah.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:48.360
AUDIENCE: So is there
any specific restrictions

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:52.330
for replicas to execute
other requests in terms

00:31:52.330 --> 00:31:56.404
of the sequence number where
it has to execute a sequence

00:31:56.404 --> 00:31:58.188
number [INAUDIBLE] execute--

00:31:58.188 --> 00:31:58.980
LISKOV: Absolutely.

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:00.480
Each replica--
non-faulty replica

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:01.800
will execute them in order.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:02.970
AUDIENCE: Okay.

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:03.807
LISKOV: Okay.

00:32:03.807 --> 00:32:04.390
AUDIENCE: So--

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:06.480
LISKOV: So non-faulty
replicas carry out

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.530
this protocol without flaw.

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:09.030
Okay.

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:10.860
So they wait to collect
the certificate.

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:11.970
They send out the message.

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:13.200
They wait to get the--

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:16.470
all those prepared certificates,
those prepared messages--

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.507
they sort of create
their commit certificate.

00:32:19.507 --> 00:32:21.090
Then, they wait to
make sure that they

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:22.475
execute the operation in order.

00:32:22.475 --> 00:32:24.600
They execute the operation
and send the answer back

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:25.210
to the client.

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:25.931
AUDIENCE: Okay.

00:32:25.931 --> 00:32:26.431
Okay.

00:32:26.431 --> 00:32:26.840
Sorry.

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:27.430
LISKOV: Yeah.

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:30.500
AUDIENCE: My other question is,
what if the primary is faulty

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:35.660
and it sends a request
to f [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:37.806
processes and
[INAUDIBLE] processes

00:32:37.806 --> 00:32:41.752
and leave the other f there?

00:32:41.752 --> 00:32:43.250
They don't execute anything.

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:47.618
And later on, how will they
keep up with the other replicas?

00:32:47.618 --> 00:32:48.160
LISKOV: Okay.

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:50.388
So first of all, there
was one too many numbers

00:32:50.388 --> 00:32:51.055
in your numbers.

00:32:51.055 --> 00:32:52.130
[LAUGHS]

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:54.520
So if the primary
is faulty, there

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:58.247
can only be f-1 other
faulty replicas.

00:32:58.247 --> 00:33:00.580
And that's important for the
algorithm to work properly,

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:03.400
because there can be f total.

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:06.160
Basically, you were
trying to ask me

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.110
what happens if
there are f failures?

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:11.140
Will this algorithm be
sufficient to guarantee

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:13.180
that the correct result obtains?

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:14.930
AUDIENCE: Basically,
I want to ask about--

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:15.890
LISKOV: Yeah.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:20.210
AUDIENCE: --some non-faulty
replicas are in some states

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:22.600
while the others
are in-- are not

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:24.520
in the same state of
these other replicas.

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:27.100
How will they later
keep up with--

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:28.850
LISKOV: I'll get to
that in just a minute.

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:29.350
Okay.

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:29.941
Yeah.

00:33:29.941 --> 00:33:31.414
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:31.414 --> 00:33:34.114
The primary as a
single point could

00:33:34.114 --> 00:33:36.324
be indicated by the client.

00:33:36.324 --> 00:33:37.810
Am I correct?

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:39.230
LISKOV: Well, actually, no.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:40.570
But it doesn't really matter.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:41.070
Okay.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:43.628
I mean, so here's what
the client actually does.

00:33:43.628 --> 00:33:45.670
There are various forms
in which you can do that.

00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:46.570
It sends it to the primary.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:47.980
If it doesn't hear
after a while,

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:48.910
it sends it to everybody.

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:49.250
AUDIENCE: Okay.

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:49.590
What if it--

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:49.800
LISKOV: Yeah.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:50.540
AUDIENCE: --sends
it to the primary--

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:51.082
LISKOV: Yeah.

00:33:51.082 --> 00:33:54.175
AUDIENCE: The primary
is a malicious hacker.

00:33:54.175 --> 00:33:55.882
And the primary
deliberately changes--

00:33:55.882 --> 00:33:57.090
let's say it's a [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:57.090 --> 00:33:57.791
LISKOV: Yup.

00:33:57.791 --> 00:33:59.166
AUDIENCE: It
deliberately changes

00:33:59.166 --> 00:34:00.250
that to modify the data.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:01.090
LISKOV: It can't.

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:01.870
AUDIENCE: And it can
do it perfectly well.

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:04.210
LISKOV: It can't do
that, because the message

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:07.090
from the client
itself is encrypted.

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:10.060
And it not only is encrypted,
but it has a client base

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:11.170
sequence number.

00:34:11.170 --> 00:34:12.490
So the replicas can tell--

00:34:12.490 --> 00:34:14.718
AUDIENCE: But it's
encrypted [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:14.718 --> 00:34:19.060
multiple encryptions
inside [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:20.590
LISKOV: If I go backwards--

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:22.219
I mean, I didn't
show you this detail.

00:34:22.219 --> 00:34:25.750
But if you look here on--

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:28.929
up here, you see
this sort of m there.

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:31.179
Well, m is something that
came from the client.

00:34:31.179 --> 00:34:34.060
And it was itself
encrypted in a way

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:35.830
that guaranteed that
only the client could

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:37.270
have produced that.

00:34:37.270 --> 00:34:38.822
And that by itself
is not sufficient.

00:34:38.822 --> 00:34:40.280
It also has a
sequence number in it

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:42.949
that guarantees that it's the
current thing the client wants

00:34:42.949 --> 00:34:43.449
to do.

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:44.910
AUDIENCE: That's
what it has to do.

00:34:44.910 --> 00:34:45.410
Okay.

00:34:45.410 --> 00:34:46.764
LISKOV: Yeah, so--

00:34:46.764 --> 00:34:47.639
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:49.750
LISKOV: So this protocol
works in various forms.

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:52.510
And one form is the client
sends this to the primary.

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:54.219
And the primary relays it.

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:57.250
Another form is the client
sends it to everybody.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.247
And all the primary has to
do is say, here's the number.

00:35:00.247 --> 00:35:02.080
And here's the digest
of the client message.

00:35:02.080 --> 00:35:04.205
But these are sort of little
optimizations of the--

00:35:04.205 --> 00:35:08.904
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] step
that's really its function

00:35:08.904 --> 00:35:10.300
and then the distribution--

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:10.800
LISKOV: Yes.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:11.612
AUDIENCE: --of everybody else.

00:35:11.612 --> 00:35:13.148
But you can't see
in the content.

00:35:13.148 --> 00:35:14.233
I mean, you can see it.

00:35:14.233 --> 00:35:15.150
LISKOV: It can see it.

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:16.450
But it can't fake it.

00:35:16.450 --> 00:35:18.310
And it can't replay it.

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:19.220
Okay.

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:19.720
Yeah.

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:21.095
AUDIENCE: There
was a presumption

00:35:21.095 --> 00:35:24.402
that the primary knows what
the current-- next sequence

00:35:24.402 --> 00:35:26.110
on there should be
something out of this.

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:26.490
LISKOV: Yes.

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:28.660
AUDIENCE: There could be
conflict between the primaries.

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:31.180
LISKOV: At any moment in time,
though, there's one primary.

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:32.860
And if there were two views--

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:34.420
there can be more than one view.

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:35.770
One would be a majority view.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:39.300
The other one would be the old
view that was sort of failing.

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:41.840
Replicas will only pay attention
to the most recent view.

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:43.465
So there's always at
any moment in time

00:35:43.465 --> 00:35:45.730
a unique replica that's
acting as the primary

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:49.851
as far as the group as
a whole is concerned.

00:35:49.851 --> 00:35:50.476
AUDIENCE: Okay.

00:35:50.476 --> 00:35:53.385
LISKOV: Okay.

00:35:53.385 --> 00:35:55.010
Now, I'm not actually
going to tell you

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:57.110
all the other protocols.

00:35:57.110 --> 00:35:59.870
What I can do is
forward you to a paper

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:03.740
that will contain all the gory
details of the rest of-- what

00:36:03.740 --> 00:36:06.560
I showed you was sort of
the normal case operation.

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:07.173
Okay.

00:36:07.173 --> 00:36:08.590
And to complete
the picture, there

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:10.850
is a bunch of other things
that have to be solved.

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:14.230
First of all, we have to
implement the view changes.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:15.020
Okay.

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:17.220
And the view changes have
to be done very carefully.

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:19.220
First of all, they have
to be done in such a way

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.620
that any operation
that committed,

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:24.080
where it was at all
possible that the client got

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:25.085
the answer--

00:36:25.085 --> 00:36:26.960
we have to make sure
that it sort of survives

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:28.020
into the next view--

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:30.480
so the very important
correctness condition there.

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:34.310
In addition, the view change
has to be done in such a way

00:36:34.310 --> 00:36:36.650
that it doesn't
interfere with liveness.

00:36:36.650 --> 00:36:40.410
So you can imagine various kinds
of problems that could arise.

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:42.800
For example, if the replicas
were too fast to do a view

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:44.870
change and suspected
the primary--

00:36:44.870 --> 00:36:46.940
or imagine that a
faulty replica tried

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:49.490
to subvert the system by
doing view changes over

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:50.645
and over again.

00:36:50.645 --> 00:36:52.520
Well, then you'd have
a system that was safe.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:53.490
You know, it did
the right thing.

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:55.500
But it never accomplished
anything at all.

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.860
So you have to have
mechanisms in the view change

00:36:57.860 --> 00:36:59.760
that ensure that
on the one hand,

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:02.090
you do view changes when
the primary is faulty.

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:04.215
And on the other hand, you
don't do them too often.

00:37:04.215 --> 00:37:04.715
Okay.

00:37:04.715 --> 00:37:06.710
And that-- those details
are all in some papers

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:09.663
that we've written
on this mechanism.

00:37:09.663 --> 00:37:12.080
Another thing is that we need
to have a garbage collection

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:13.220
technique in here.

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:15.080
I didn't show you how
we were accumulating

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:16.440
all these certificates.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:19.080
But the fact is we're
storing them into a log.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:22.320
And you can't let that
log grow without bound.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:26.450
And so there has to be a way of
shrinking the log periodically

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:28.040
so that it doesn't get too big.

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:30.040
And this system has to
also be done carefully.

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:31.790
You actually have to
carry out a protocol,

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:33.873
where the replicas agree
that it's okay to get rid

00:37:33.873 --> 00:37:35.210
of the tail of the log.

00:37:35.210 --> 00:37:37.190
Otherwise, in certain
view change situations,

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:40.190
you wouldn't be able to go
ahead and form the new view.

00:37:40.190 --> 00:37:42.655
And then, the state transfer
answers the question

00:37:42.655 --> 00:37:44.030
that somebody
asked a moment ago.

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:46.570
What happens if I have a
perfectly good replica,

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:48.930
but it somehow or other
wasn't in the act?

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:51.330
You know, and so it didn't
hear about what was going on.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.100
How does it bring
itself up to date?

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:56.330
And we have a very efficient
state transfer protocol

00:37:56.330 --> 00:37:58.070
for bringing
replicas up to date.

00:37:58.070 --> 00:38:01.010
The big problem with bringing
slow replicas up to date

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:03.710
is that when you execute
operations and delete

00:38:03.710 --> 00:38:05.300
information from
the log, you have

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:08.390
a potentially huge database that
contains all the changes that

00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:09.860
have happened in the past.

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:13.160
And you don't want to have to
ship that whole database off

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:16.250
when a slow replica needs to
find out information in it.

00:38:16.250 --> 00:38:18.260
So we have a very nice
tree structure sort

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:21.630
of hierarchical system, where
at high levels of the tree,

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:24.770
we have digests that represent
all the information below them.

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:26.787
And the leaves of the
tree are the pages,

00:38:26.787 --> 00:38:28.370
whatever the smallest
unit is that you

00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:29.990
want to actually transfer.

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:32.330
And we're able to carry
out a protocol for this

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:34.382
that allows us to send
just the pages that

00:38:34.382 --> 00:38:36.590
need to be sent in order to
bring the slow replica up

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:37.090
to date.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:41.140
Okay.

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:44.290
So this is what I would
call the base algorithm.

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:48.460
This is BFT, Byzantine Fault
Tolerance, the base algorithm.

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:51.533
Oh, before I go on, there are
a whole bunch of optimizations.

00:38:51.533 --> 00:38:52.450
I've alluded to these.

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:54.370
Some of these are not
necessarily optimizations.

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:56.130
They're pessimizations,
because you aren't sure

00:38:56.130 --> 00:38:58.338
until you run it, you know,
what's going to be better

00:38:58.338 --> 00:38:59.660
and what's going to be worse.

00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:02.763
But here are the kinds of things
that you can imagine doing.

00:39:02.763 --> 00:39:04.930
For example, it isn't really
necessary for everybody

00:39:04.930 --> 00:39:06.640
to send a reply to the client.

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:10.575
It's okay if all of but one of
you send digests of the reply,

00:39:10.575 --> 00:39:12.700
because this will be
sufficient to allow the client

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:16.690
to tell whether it
has identical replies.

00:39:16.690 --> 00:39:18.910
We've talked already about
this request transmission.

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:20.410
It isn't necessary
for the client

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:22.030
to send the message
with the primary.

00:39:22.030 --> 00:39:23.470
And the primary relays it.

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:25.330
The client can send
every-- to everybody.

00:39:25.330 --> 00:39:30.070
And the primary can just send
a digest along in the messages.

00:39:30.070 --> 00:39:32.390
Also, in our system,
we do batching.

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:35.230
So the primary-- if you imagine
a very busy primary that's

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:37.720
getting hit with request
after request after request,

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:40.270
it doesn't actually start the
protocol for each request.

00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:42.280
Instead, it collects
a batch of requests

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:44.710
and does one protocol
for the bunch of them.

00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:47.270
And then, optimistic
execution-- this one, I think,

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:49.810
is the one that's
the pessimization.

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:52.270
The idea here is
that you might reply

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:55.450
to a read request before
you've actually executed,

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:56.920
so sort of out of order.

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:58.180
It's optimistic.

00:39:58.180 --> 00:39:59.830
And with optimistic
stuff, there's

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:02.420
always a question of
whether it was worthwhile

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:04.030
if the answer is wrong.

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:06.970
It requires that the primary
collect 2f+1 answers rather

00:40:06.970 --> 00:40:08.060
than f+1.

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:09.697
So there's a cost for it.

00:40:09.697 --> 00:40:12.280
And sometimes you have to take
it back, because it wasn't just

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:14.410
the right thing to do.

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:14.910
Oh.

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:17.798
AUDIENCE: You mentinoed in the
previous slide transparency.

00:40:17.798 --> 00:40:18.340
LISKOV: Yeah.

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:20.132
AUDIENCE: Can you make
a precise statements

00:40:20.132 --> 00:40:22.685
about the sensitivity
of this to-- tends

00:40:22.685 --> 00:40:24.872
to slow things down
or deny service

00:40:24.872 --> 00:40:27.788
by a primary [INAUDIBLE]
coming up and down?

00:40:27.788 --> 00:40:31.190
Is it here or not here?

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:33.640
[INAUDIBLE] view changes--
things like that.

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:36.610
LISKOV: So the algorithm has
in it very clever techniques

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:39.100
for doing this sort
of little dance

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:40.630
between doing things
too frequently

00:40:40.630 --> 00:40:43.528
and doing things too slowly.

00:40:43.528 --> 00:40:45.070
And if you look at
Miguel's' thesis--

00:40:45.070 --> 00:40:48.190
and I will put up a slide
later saying who Miguel is--

00:40:48.190 --> 00:40:50.190
he has a quite careful
analysis of some of this.

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:51.982
But I don't think it's
at the level of what

00:40:51.982 --> 00:40:53.110
you're talking about.

00:40:53.110 --> 00:40:56.260
These systems are
very hard to analyze.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.350
And I doubt that we
have a precise answer

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:00.520
to that particular question.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:07.130
Okay.

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:07.630
All right.

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:09.830
So the system that I've
described you so far,

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:13.980
this sort of base system,
has a major problem.

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:14.480
Okay.

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:17.450
It allows a system to
provide good service,

00:41:17.450 --> 00:41:19.880
provided it has no more than
f failures over the system

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:20.990
lifetime.

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:23.520
But systems today
last for a long time.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:26.750
And so you know for a fact that
over the lifetime of a system,

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:28.838
there's going to be more
than one failure or more

00:41:28.838 --> 00:41:29.505
than f failures.

00:41:29.505 --> 00:41:32.870
AUDIENCE: The total lifetime or
just [INAUDIBLE] at one time?

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:35.210
LISKOV: Over the
lifetime-- okay.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:37.890
So we have to close that gap.

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:42.620
And so we need a system that
does exactly what Ron just

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:44.690
said-- namely, it
doesn't have more than f

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:46.500
simultaneous failures.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.833
And we have to do
this in a system--

00:41:48.833 --> 00:41:50.750
in a world in which we
can't tell who's faulty

00:41:50.750 --> 00:41:51.847
and who's not.

00:41:51.847 --> 00:41:53.930
So the big difference--
one of the big differences

00:41:53.930 --> 00:41:55.820
in this world versus
the simpler world I

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:57.528
talked about at the
beginning of the talk

00:41:57.528 --> 00:41:59.210
is you simply can't tell.

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:02.210
A faulty replica may appear
to be perfectly okay,

00:42:02.210 --> 00:42:04.700
because it's answering
queries and acting normally.

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:06.890
A non-faulty
replica might appear

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:08.930
to not be okay, because
for example, there's

00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:10.590
some sort of denial
of service attack.

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:12.930
And it's not able to get its
messages through right now.

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:16.040
So you have no way of knowing
a priori whether a replica is

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:17.810
faulty or not.

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:20.750
So we're doing something that
we call proactive recovery.

00:42:20.750 --> 00:42:22.790
The idea is we're going
to periodically recover

00:42:22.790 --> 00:42:25.980
these replicas, whether
they're faulty or not.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:28.160
And if they were faulty,
the recovery procedure

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:30.150
will correct them.

00:42:30.150 --> 00:42:30.650
Okay.

00:42:30.650 --> 00:42:33.150
And that's how we're going to
get f simultaneous failures as

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:35.570
opposed to f failures over
the lifetime of system.

00:42:35.570 --> 00:42:38.840
But the recovery has to
be done very carefully

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:40.490
since if the replica
isn't faulty,

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:41.990
we want to make
sure that we don't

00:42:41.990 --> 00:42:45.110
make it appear to be faulty
and that cause things to fail.

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:48.330
And given this technique
that I'm about to describe,

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.210
we can provide correct
service, provided

00:42:50.210 --> 00:42:53.960
no more than f failures occur
in some small time window,

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:55.370
where small is on
the order of 10

00:42:55.370 --> 00:42:59.370
to 20 minutes in the current
way that we run the system.

00:42:59.370 --> 00:43:01.190
So what you're
going to want to do

00:43:01.190 --> 00:43:03.770
is you're going to want to
think about your system, how

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:05.360
vulnerable it is to failures.

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:08.300
And then, you'll select
a replication number

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:10.595
that gives you confidence
that given this window,

00:43:10.595 --> 00:43:11.720
that's good enough for you.

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:17.360
Okay.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:21.080
Now, the way that we
do proactive recovery

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:25.310
is by equipping every replica
with a secure co-processor.

00:43:25.310 --> 00:43:27.920
And the secure
co-processor stores

00:43:27.920 --> 00:43:29.820
the replica's private key.

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:31.100
So the idea is you can't--

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:33.140
basically, the idea of
the secure co-processor

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:35.330
is it's not possible
to corrupt or get

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:37.730
at the information in
the secure co-processor

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:40.280
without physical presence.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:43.050
You can't get there by
hacking in over the internet.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:43.550
Okay.

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:46.280
So the secure co-processor
stores the private key.

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:48.332
And associated with
it is a watchdog timer

00:43:48.332 --> 00:43:50.540
that's going to go off
periodically and tell you when

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:52.280
to do the proactive recovery.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:54.530
And furthermore, there's
a read-only memory

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:56.180
that stores the code.

00:43:56.180 --> 00:43:57.320
Okay.

00:43:57.320 --> 00:44:00.290
So the secure co-processor,
when the timer goes off,

00:44:00.290 --> 00:44:02.220
is going to restart the node.

00:44:02.220 --> 00:44:04.880
And as I said before,
the recovery protocol

00:44:04.880 --> 00:44:06.440
has to be done in
such a way that it

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:09.200
works whether the replica
was faulty or not.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:09.890
Okay.

00:44:09.890 --> 00:44:13.130
And a correct replica has
to be able to keep its state

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:14.370
and continue processing.

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:17.060
Otherwise, you can turn a
perfectly functioning system

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:20.030
into one that doesn't function.

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:22.950
So I'm just going to walk
through the steps here.

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:23.450
Okay.

00:44:23.450 --> 00:44:26.733
So now, imagine that
the timer went off.

00:44:26.733 --> 00:44:29.150
And the secure co-processor
said to the replica, it's time

00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:30.210
to recover.

00:44:30.210 --> 00:44:30.710
Okay.

00:44:30.710 --> 00:44:32.418
So the first thing
the replica gets to do

00:44:32.418 --> 00:44:34.100
is it gets to save its state--

00:44:34.100 --> 00:44:35.580
okay-- write it to disk.

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:37.770
And this is a timed
operation, because of course,

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:40.770
a faulty replica might
refuse to cooperate.

00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:43.490
So you simply allow enough
time for a non-faulty replica

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:44.588
to save its state.

00:44:44.588 --> 00:44:46.130
And then, you're
going to recover it,

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:47.420
whether it wants to or not.

00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:49.628
In the case of a non-faulty
replica, it won't matter.

00:44:49.628 --> 00:44:51.170
It was faulty anyway.

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:52.250
Okay.

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:54.470
As soon as that time
period has come up,

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:58.670
the secure co-processor
causes the system to reboot.

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:02.580
And it reloads the code
from this read-only memory.

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:04.820
And then, you can get
the state off the disk.

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:07.188
At this point, the replica
has the correct code,

00:45:07.188 --> 00:45:09.230
because you took it off
of this read-only memory.

00:45:09.230 --> 00:45:11.720
And if it was correct, it has
the correct state, as well.

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:14.510
If it was faulty, it has
whatever garbage it had.

00:45:14.510 --> 00:45:15.612
Okay.

00:45:15.612 --> 00:45:17.570
Now, the next thing that
happens is the replica

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:20.420
immediately changes
its secret keys,

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:23.270
because if this was an
attack, the attacker may

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:25.160
have stolen all the keys.

00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:27.290
And we need to be
very careful not

00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:30.500
to allow some node that's
not a member of the system

00:45:30.500 --> 00:45:31.650
to impersonate.

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:32.150
Okay.

00:45:32.150 --> 00:45:34.610
So if the keys are
stolen, some node

00:45:34.610 --> 00:45:36.440
that's not one of
the replicas could

00:45:36.440 --> 00:45:38.690
pretend to be a
replica, because it

00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:41.310
would know the right keys
to use for communication.

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:43.380
And if that happened,
all bets are off,

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:45.740
because all of a sudden, we
have more than f replicas

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:48.380
since these other nodes
are impersonating replicas.

00:45:48.380 --> 00:45:51.140
So we change the
keys that this node

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:54.650
will allow to be used for the
messages that are coming to it.

00:45:54.650 --> 00:45:57.170
That's an action it
can take unilaterally.

00:45:57.170 --> 00:45:59.790
And then, it sends
out a recovery request

00:45:59.790 --> 00:46:02.250
to all the other replicas,
acting as a client.

00:46:02.250 --> 00:46:02.750
Okay.

00:46:02.750 --> 00:46:04.542
So it's simply saying
to the other replicas

00:46:04.542 --> 00:46:07.290
as if it was a client-- it
says, I want to recover.

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:08.480
Okay.

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:11.570
And when the other
replicas get this message,

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:14.150
they immediately change
their secret keys.

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:17.660
So that's the other side
of the impersonation story.

00:46:17.660 --> 00:46:20.090
They won't accept messages
from these other replicas,

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:22.820
these impersonators either.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:25.310
And through a
mechanism that I'm not

00:46:25.310 --> 00:46:27.800
going be able to tell you
about in today's talk,

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:33.110
they then establish a
sequence number by which time

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:35.250
the recovery will be complete.

00:46:35.250 --> 00:46:38.010
So they're going to say, you
know, right now, it's at n.

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:40.342
At n+100, the recovery
will be complete.

00:46:40.342 --> 00:46:42.800
And the protocol is going to
ensure that this will actually

00:46:42.800 --> 00:46:48.330
be true by the time
you get to n+100.

00:46:48.330 --> 00:46:48.830
Okay.

00:46:48.830 --> 00:46:51.650
Now, after this
point, the replicas

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:54.200
just continue doing their
normal protocol, including

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:55.860
this replica, this recovery.

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:58.460
And this is very important,
because if it wasn't faulty,

00:46:58.460 --> 00:47:01.340
it needs to act like
a non-faulty replica.

00:47:01.340 --> 00:47:02.810
Otherwise, the
system could stall.

00:47:02.810 --> 00:47:06.830
Or you might have
too many failures.

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:09.440
And meanwhile, the
recovery replica

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:13.370
is checking its state using
these digests that I talk about

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:14.840
in communicating
with other nodes

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:17.330
to find out whether its
state is the same as what

00:47:17.330 --> 00:47:19.790
the other replicas think
its state ought to be.

00:47:19.790 --> 00:47:21.902
And if it discovers that
it's missing some state,

00:47:21.902 --> 00:47:23.360
then it's going to
fetch them using

00:47:23.360 --> 00:47:27.350
this protocol that I mentioned
of the tree structured thing.

00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:33.188
And by the time we get to n+100,
the recovery will be complete.

00:47:33.188 --> 00:47:35.480
And the recovery replica will
have recovered its state,

00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:36.650
as well.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:38.690
So that's the basic idea.

00:47:38.690 --> 00:47:40.505
Am I running out of time?

00:47:40.505 --> 00:47:41.630
AUDIENCE: Got five minutes.

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:42.260
LISKOV: Okay.

00:47:42.260 --> 00:47:43.120
Good.

00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:43.620
All right.

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:47.430
So I'm going to
finish up very fast.

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:48.992
We did some performance
experiments.

00:47:48.992 --> 00:47:50.450
Basically, we
implemented something

00:47:50.450 --> 00:47:53.330
called BFT, this little
library that anybody who wants

00:47:53.330 --> 00:47:54.115
can get hold of.

00:47:54.115 --> 00:47:56.240
And then, we used it to
implement a replicated file

00:47:56.240 --> 00:47:56.740
system.

00:47:56.740 --> 00:48:01.620
We did NFS, which is
a common file system.

00:48:01.620 --> 00:48:03.990
And we ran it on the
Andrew benchmark,

00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:06.720
which is a standard file system
benchmark, except it's old.

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:10.680
And therefore, we made it
much bigger than as originally

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:11.280
defined.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:13.170
So we run something
called Andrew100,

00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:15.990
which is 100 times bigger than
Andrew and the Andrew500, which

00:48:15.990 --> 00:48:18.240
is 500 times bigger than Andrew.

00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:21.030
And we run it on a
bunch of machines.

00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:23.850
And in particular, we're
doing the four replicas

00:48:23.850 --> 00:48:26.580
to survive one failure case.

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:27.780
Okay.

00:48:27.780 --> 00:48:30.300
And here's our results.

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:32.220
This is Andrew100.

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:37.260
And what you can
see is that we ran

00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:40.035
three versions of the system
BFS with the full fault

00:48:40.035 --> 00:48:41.130
tolerance turned on--

00:48:41.130 --> 00:48:44.293
no replica, which doesn't
have any replication.

00:48:44.293 --> 00:48:45.960
So we're just running
the bear algorithm

00:48:45.960 --> 00:48:48.930
on a single machine, and
then the base system.

00:48:48.930 --> 00:48:51.660
And what you see here is an
interesting truth about NFS.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:54.330
First of all, you may have
thought that it synced to disk

00:48:54.330 --> 00:48:56.900
when you wrote something
to a file, but it doesn't.

00:48:56.900 --> 00:48:57.400
Okay.

00:48:57.400 --> 00:48:59.220
And that's why BFS--

00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:03.637
No Replica-- No Replica and
NFS have identical performance.

00:49:03.637 --> 00:49:05.970
You know, our system, No
Replica, doesn't write to disk,

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:07.350
because we're going
to rely on replication

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:08.517
to take care of the problem.

00:49:08.517 --> 00:49:10.380
We're writing to disk
in the background.

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:11.130
Okay.

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:12.895
And we have identical
performance to NFS.

00:49:12.895 --> 00:49:15.020
And that's because it's
not writing to disk either.

00:49:15.020 --> 00:49:16.200
Okay.

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:16.710
All right.

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:18.360
BFS is slower.

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:21.750
And actually, in this particular
case, it's about 20% slower.

00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:26.370
And the same thing
is true in Andrew500.

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:29.760
So we've run these experiments
with both with Interfast

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:31.260
and also with Solaris.

00:49:31.260 --> 00:49:34.380
And our results show that we
run anywhere from 2% faster

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:35.920
to 20% slower.

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:38.070
We're faster than Solaris,
because Solaris actually

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:39.820
writes to disk.

00:49:39.820 --> 00:49:41.310
Okay.

00:49:41.310 --> 00:49:44.390
So we have good performance.

00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:47.890
And I don't really have
time to talk about this.

00:49:47.890 --> 00:49:50.050
But we have actually
defined some future--

00:49:50.050 --> 00:49:51.940
some further work
that allows us to get

00:49:51.940 --> 00:49:54.400
rid of some of these
requirements of state machine

00:49:54.400 --> 00:49:58.750
replication, deterministic
operations in particular.

00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:01.390
And also, the algorithm
I described to you

00:50:01.390 --> 00:50:04.600
relies on every replica
having identical state.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:07.080
And that can be a problem
if your implementations

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:08.500
are non-deterministic.

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:10.018
Or furthermore, you
might like to be

00:50:10.018 --> 00:50:11.560
able to run different
implementations

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:13.000
of the different replicas.

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:14.830
So those were limitations.

00:50:14.830 --> 00:50:20.520
And we have an extension
to BFT called base that

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:24.915
overcomes those limitations
by combining Byzantine fault

00:50:24.915 --> 00:50:27.270
tolerance with a kind
of data abstraction

00:50:27.270 --> 00:50:29.430
that even allows us
to run implementations

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:31.570
that have slightly
different specifications.

00:50:31.570 --> 00:50:33.240
And so the system
as a whole satisfies

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:34.740
an abstract specification.

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:38.610
So this gives us what we
call opportunistic inversion

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:39.840
programming.

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:41.533
Inversion programming
is the only way

00:50:41.533 --> 00:50:43.950
people have ever been able to
suggest that would allow you

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:45.570
to really survive
software failures,

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:48.900
because you can run
different implementations.

00:50:48.900 --> 00:50:51.540
But the trouble
is that you really

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:53.580
can't start off from
scratch to write four

00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:55.030
implementations of the system.

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:57.030
If you take off the shelf
implementations, which

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:58.980
are available for
important services,

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:01.350
the chances are they don't
do exactly the same thing.

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:04.060
But this technique will
mask their differences.

00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:07.390
And so we can get
very nice behavior.

00:51:07.390 --> 00:51:12.310
And as I mentioned a moment
ago, there are co-authors.

00:51:12.310 --> 00:51:14.310
In particular, this work
has been done primarily

00:51:14.310 --> 00:51:15.440
with two students--

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:18.840
Miguel Castro, who did
the original work on BFT

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:22.170
and finished his
PhD about a year ago

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:26.070
and Rodrigo Rodrigues, who
worked on the base system

00:51:26.070 --> 00:51:28.920
along with me and Miguel.

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:36.630
So in conclusion, we have an
algorithm that's practical.

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:38.520
And not only that, but
it really provides you

00:51:38.520 --> 00:51:39.978
with the kind of
behavior you want,

00:51:39.978 --> 00:51:42.313
because these
systems can survive,

00:51:42.313 --> 00:51:43.980
provided they're no
more than f failures

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:46.050
in a small time window.

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:48.510
And there's a bunch
of future work

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:51.870
that we may or may
not decide to do.

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:55.020
By reconfiguration, I mean,
when I talked about replicas,

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:56.632
there was a fixed
set of replicas.

00:51:56.632 --> 00:51:58.590
Supposing that you'd like
to remove one and put

00:51:58.590 --> 00:52:01.680
another one in, how do you
manage to make that work?

00:52:01.680 --> 00:52:03.660
I thought proactive
recovery would be simple

00:52:03.660 --> 00:52:06.120
when I started to work on
it-- turned out to be hard.

00:52:06.120 --> 00:52:08.790
So I wouldn't be surprised if
reconfiguration is likewise

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:10.710
quite hard.

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:13.425
Scalability-- I'm
really more interested

00:52:13.425 --> 00:52:14.800
here in the question
of supposing

00:52:14.800 --> 00:52:17.610
have a great big
system, how do I insert

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:22.410
the right sets of replicas
to make the system perform

00:52:22.410 --> 00:52:24.060
with advantages
that BFT gives you

00:52:24.060 --> 00:52:26.700
without having to provide, say,
four copies of every server

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:28.020
in the system?

00:52:28.020 --> 00:52:30.570
And it would be interesting
to look at performance

00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:33.180
under attack, supposing there's
a denial of service attack.

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:34.890
Or there's really
a vicious hacker.

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:36.510
In the performance
phase I showed you,

00:52:36.510 --> 00:52:38.070
we're just proactively
recovering.

00:52:38.070 --> 00:52:39.960
But we aren't trying
to simulate what

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:42.600
would happen if there were
real attacks in the system,

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:45.690
although I must say that
with the proactive recovery,

00:52:45.690 --> 00:52:48.570
because of the fact that we run
these systems under heavy load,

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:50.730
just the very act of
recovering a replica means

00:52:50.730 --> 00:52:51.960
it loses a lot of state.

00:52:51.960 --> 00:52:53.430
So it's way behind.

00:52:53.430 --> 00:52:55.355
And therefore, we
are already seeing

00:52:55.355 --> 00:52:56.730
sort of the cost
of what it would

00:52:56.730 --> 00:53:00.160
be to bring a faulty
replica back up to date.

00:53:00.160 --> 00:53:00.660
Okay.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:03.112
So that's it.

00:53:03.112 --> 00:53:05.430
Yeah.

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:08.590
AUDIENCE: You showed that it
was about a 20% performance hit,

00:53:08.590 --> 00:53:10.240
because I think it was four?

00:53:10.240 --> 00:53:11.440
LISKOV: Yes.

00:53:11.440 --> 00:53:14.290
AUDIENCE: If it were 10 or
20, would it also be 20%?

00:53:16.940 --> 00:53:17.987
LISKOV: I can't remember.

00:53:17.987 --> 00:53:19.820
I mean, these results
are in Miguel's thesis

00:53:19.820 --> 00:53:21.590
up to some number of replicas.

00:53:21.590 --> 00:53:23.990
He might not have
gone beyond seven.

00:53:23.990 --> 00:53:26.270
The truth is people
don't typically

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:29.717
think of running systems like
this with more than just a--

00:53:29.717 --> 00:53:31.925
to survive more than a very
small number of failures.

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:38.300
I can't invent the
analysis for you.

00:53:38.300 --> 00:53:39.592
It's kind of what you'd expect.

00:53:39.592 --> 00:53:42.092
You know, there's going to be--
the more replicas there are,

00:53:42.092 --> 00:53:43.332
the more messages there are.

00:53:43.332 --> 00:53:46.430
And these will tend to interfere
with normal case processing.

00:53:46.430 --> 00:53:48.638
So you would expect
a degradation.

00:53:48.638 --> 00:53:50.180
If I think about
his graph, I believe

00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:52.250
the degradation is not huge.

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:55.700
It's kind of a small
linear sort of degradation.

00:53:55.700 --> 00:53:58.070
But I should've brought
his thesis with me.

00:53:58.070 --> 00:53:58.640
But I didn't.

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:01.993
AUDIENCE: Would it impact
the more exponential growth

00:54:01.993 --> 00:54:03.637
messages?

00:54:03.637 --> 00:54:06.220
LISKOV: Yes, but there's a lot
of stuff going on in the system

00:54:06.220 --> 00:54:09.440
to optimize, you know,
like the group-- you know,

00:54:09.440 --> 00:54:12.370
handling of groups and stuff
like that that offset to some

00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:14.750
extent the kinds of
problems that you would--

00:54:14.750 --> 00:54:18.730
you know, you might have fewer,
bigger messages, for example.

00:54:18.730 --> 00:54:20.570
But yes, absolutely.

00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:21.070
Yeah.

00:54:21.070 --> 00:54:23.749
AUDIENCE: It just occurred to me
that he used cryptographic keys

00:54:23.749 --> 00:54:23.922
here.

00:54:23.922 --> 00:54:25.630
Which parties have
which keys [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:28.628
share what your [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:28.628 --> 00:54:29.170
LISKOV: Okay.

00:54:29.170 --> 00:54:30.045
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:30.045 --> 00:54:31.620
LISKOV: So I didn't
show you anything

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:33.910
having to do with
the private keys.

00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:34.410
Okay.

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:37.750
Everything I'm showing you
here is using secret keys.

00:54:37.750 --> 00:54:38.270
Okay.

00:54:38.270 --> 00:54:38.770
And--

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:39.790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:39.790 --> 00:54:40.323
LISKOV: Hmm?

00:54:40.323 --> 00:54:41.740
AUDIENCE: Conventional
symmetric--

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:44.230
LISKOV: Conventional
symmetric cryptography--

00:54:44.230 --> 00:54:49.490
we used the public keys to
establish the secret keys.

00:54:49.490 --> 00:54:49.990
Okay.

00:54:49.990 --> 00:54:51.370
And that's done on recovery.

00:54:51.370 --> 00:54:53.960
And it's also actually
done every 30 seconds.

00:54:53.960 --> 00:54:56.410
So we're refreshing those
secret keys all the time,

00:54:56.410 --> 00:54:59.140
using the private keys.

00:54:59.140 --> 00:54:59.730
Okay.

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:03.500
AUDIENCE: So each client has
a key that it shares with--

00:55:03.500 --> 00:55:04.340
LISKOV: One replica.

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:05.257
AUDIENCE: One replica.

00:55:05.257 --> 00:55:06.050
LISKOV: Right.

00:55:06.050 --> 00:55:07.655
And each replica
has a key that it

00:55:07.655 --> 00:55:09.988
shares with-- in fact, each
replica has two keys that it

00:55:09.988 --> 00:55:12.620
shares with another replica--
one for sending messages

00:55:12.620 --> 00:55:14.450
and one for receiving messages.

00:55:14.450 --> 00:55:17.390
That was why replicas could
unilaterally throw away

00:55:17.390 --> 00:55:20.330
the keys that they used
for receiving messages,

00:55:20.330 --> 00:55:22.850
since they could
still send messages,

00:55:22.850 --> 00:55:24.600
but they couldn't receive.

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:27.012
Yeah, so there's a lot of
secret keys floating around,

00:55:27.012 --> 00:55:29.220
and they're being refreshed
periodically and actually

00:55:29.220 --> 00:55:31.137
at a pretty fast clip.

00:55:31.137 --> 00:55:31.970
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:32.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:32.470 --> 00:55:33.820
LISKOV: Yeah.

